# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Foundry Parent App — root environment (copy to .env)
#   cp .env.example .env   then fill in the values marked <…>
# The server package reads this file (packages/server loads ../../.env).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ---- Parent API server ----
# Host ports form one block: 4000 API, 4001 WS, 4002 web, 4003 mcp-bridge.
# Data stores keep +1 offsets (5433/6380).
PORT=                    # REST API
# NOT legacy. `/ws/generation` was deleted in Phase 4, but this port now serves
# `/ws/flow-execution` — live per-step run progress, authorised by a single-use
# ticket minted by POST …/flow-executions/:id/watch (D-15). Unset it and the run
# page falls back to polling: same data, slower. Do not remove the port. It is 4001, set by infra/docker-compose.yml
# (and the server's default) — not here: an empty `WS_PORT=` here read as port 0 on a host-run server.
# NODE_ENV is set to production by the compose file for every container; an empty one here is not "unset".

# Simple API key guarding the parent's own API (send as x-api-key).
PARENT_API_KEY=

# mcp-bridge's own credential (send as x-api-key to :4003). Deliberately NOT
# PARENT_API_KEY — mcp-bridge is a standalone package with no dependency on
# packages/server or @foundry/shared, and has never shared a secret with
# either. Read directly by packages/mcp-bridge (see its own .env.example);
# listed here too because docker-compose passes it through from this file.
MCP_BRIDGE_API_KEY=

# ---- Postgres (parent app store) ----
# Matches infra/docker-compose.yml (host port 5433).
DATABASE_URL=

# ---- No REDIS_URL ----
# There was one, "reserved for BullMQ / scale", for as long as there was a Redis
# container: empty, with no client library and no reader. Both are gone
# (GAPS.md 6.4). A reserved variable in the example file is how the next person
# concludes the queue is already wired up.

# ---- Execution budgets (M3/M4) — fallbacks when a workspace has no row ----
# Deliberately finite. "Unlimited when unconfigured" makes the runaway guard
# opt-in, and the workspace that starves the instance is the unconfigured one.
BUDGET_DEFAULT_MAX_CONCURRENT=
# Unset = token spend not metered. Distinct from 0, which would refuse everything.
#BUDGET_DEFAULT_MAX_TOKENS_PER_DAY=

# ---- Credential encryption (AES-256-GCM) for datasource secrets at rest ----
# Generate a real one: openssl rand -hex 32
CREDENTIAL_ENC_KEY=

# ---- Which hosts a datasource may be registered against (GAPS.md 1.3) ----
# Comma-separated. `db.example.com` matches exactly; `.example.com` matches
# anything under it. UNSET means this deployment has not expressed an opinion,
# which the refusal verdict reports rather than passing silently.
#
# ⚠ This is the WEAKER half and must never be described as the control: unset it
# cannot fail, and set it is walked past by spelling the same database a
# different way (`postgres`, `127.0.0.1`, the container IP, a pgbouncer). What
# actually refuses a datasource pointed at Foundry's own state is the identity
# and signature check in services/datasource-target.service.ts, which asks the
# target who it is and has no address in it at all. See D-64.
#DATASOURCE_HOST_ALLOWLIST=

# ---- The demo tenant databases: Finance and Legal ----
#
# ⚠ EVERY VARIABLE IN THIS BLOCK IS READ TODAY AND WAS DOCUMENTED NOWHERE UNTIL
# now. `seed-finance-db.ts` has read the three FINANCE_* names since Programme 3
# Stage 1; the LEGAL_* trio arrived with the Legal domain. All six are OPTIONAL —
# each falls back to the default shown — so nothing was broken by the omission,
# which is exactly why it went unnoticed.
#
# The database and role names only need setting to run a second copy of a tenant
# side by side on one cluster.
#FINANCE_DB_NAME=finance_demo
#FINANCE_DB_RO_ROLE=finance_ro
#LEGAL_DB_NAME=legal_demo
#LEGAL_DB_RO_ROLE=legal_ro
#
# ⚠ The read-only passwords are DERIVED from CREDENTIAL_ENC_KEY (HMAC-SHA256
# under a per-tenant salt), not generated per seed. That is deliberate: a reseed
# does not go through provisioning, so a fresh random password each time would
# leave the DSN already encrypted into `datasources.connection_string_enc`
# pointing at a credential the server had just rotated away — and every query
# would then fail to authenticate for a reason nothing reports.
#
# Setting either variable below is therefore how you rotate DELIBERATELY. Do it
# and then re-run the matching `*:seed`, which re-registers the datasource.
#FINANCE_DB_RO_PASSWORD=
#LEGAL_DB_RO_PASSWORD=

# ---- LLM provider for the `llm_complete` tool (Stage 6) ----
# ⚠ LLM_BASE_URL and LLM_API_KEY are REQUIRED — config/index.ts's required()
# throws at import time if either is unset or empty, so @foundry/server cannot
# boot (or even load in a test file that imports anything on the config chain)
# without them. This comment used to say "OPTIONAL and only a FALLBACK" and
# that is no longer true at the code level: what's still true is that the LIVE
# model connection is set on the AI models page (Models) and stored in
# Foundry's `runtime_model_provider`. It is the one connection for the whole
# deployment (workflows, the builder and Forge). These values are what it falls
# back to when nothing has been saved, not something you can leave unset.
#
# ⚠ Read before setting: this is a BILLABLE credential. It is bounded (operator-set host, model allowlist, size and
# per-minute caps) rather than trusted. OpenAI-compatible; the URL ends at the
# root that serves /chat/completions and /models. For local dev/test with no
# real gateway, any non-empty placeholder satisfies required() — the fallback
# is only actually dialed if a workspace never configures its own provider.
LLM_BASE_URL=
LLM_API_KEY=
# The fallback model for everything (workflows, the builder, Forge) until models
# are enabled on the AI models page. The old names LLM_REASONING_MODEL and
# LLM_FAST_MODEL are still read, in that order, when LLM_MODEL is unset.
LLM_MODEL=

# ── Identity (Stage 9.1) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The secret the sign-in flow signs a user identity with and the parent API
# verifies. A SECOND secret on purpose — PARENT_API_KEY authorises a request,
# this one attests a person. Sharing them would mean anyone holding the API key
# could name any approver, which is exactly what Stage 9 ends (I-13).
#
# Unset means the routes that record a person fail closed with a 503 naming this
# variable. Failing open would leave an approval recording a name nothing
# checked, which is the state being left behind.
RUNTIME_JWT_SECRET=
# Template for local environment variables.
# Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in real values. NEVER commit .env.
# Every variable your code reads must be documented here with a placeholder.
#
# Example entries - replace with your project's actual variables:
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/mydb
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-api-key-here
# AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING=your-connection-string-here

# ---- Super admin — ⚠ LOAD-BEARING, NOT OPTIONAL ----
# Comma-separated identity **subjects or emails**. A super admin has access to
# every workspace at the highest role, member or not, and is the ONLY identity
# that may create/rename/delete a workspace or assign somebody to one.
#
# ⚠ EMPTY IS NOT "the feature off" ANY MORE. There used to be a self-service
# bootstrap — the first signed-in caller could claim any unclaimed workspace and
# become its admin — and it is deleted, because it meant whoever asked first
# owned the tenant. Assignment replaced it. So with this unset, **no workspace
# can be created and no memberless workspace can ever get a member**, and the
# UI's Manage Workspaces page is unreachable by everyone. The product says so
# rather than looking broken: the page, `GET /api/me` and every 403 name this
# variable when it is empty.
#
# ⚠ SUBJECT **OR** EMAIL, and this used to be subject-only despite the name.
# An identity's subject is its token's `sub`: for a Microsoft Entra sign-in that
# is the OID (a GUID), so a deployment that listed emails — i.e. did what this
# variable is called — granted nothing at all, silently. Either now matches
# (email case-insensitively, subject exactly).
#
# It is an ACCESS grant only: the accountability gates (self-approval refusal,
# run-requester refusal, provenance escalation) still apply to a super admin.
# ⚠ It is NOT separation of duties — anything holding RUNTIME_JWT_SECRET can
# mint any identity, so read it as "the deployment's operators".
#
# ⚠ Server-side env, deliberately: it is the one authority in the product that
# cannot be granted from inside the product, so nobody handed workspace
# administration can also hand out the power to administer.
# ⚠ **AND SINCE 2026-09-13 IT IS THE ONLY WAY INTO A WORKSPACE THAT HAS NO MEMBERS.**
# The RBAC feature closed 106 previously unguarded workspace-scoped routes, so a
# workspace with no members now refuses EVERY non-super-admin on every one of them —
# not just approvals. With `POST …/members/claim` deleted, an allowlist holder is the
# only caller who passes without a membership row, which is what lets them grant the
# first one. On a deployment with no members and an empty allowlist, nobody can do
# anything in any workspace and there is no way to fix it from inside the product.
# ⚠ Name only — never a value in this file (Constitution VIII).
SUPER_ADMIN_EMAILS=

# ── Real email delivery: Microsoft Graph sendMail (email-send-tool) ──────────
# The `foundry_email_send` tool. ⚠ This is the ONLY capability in Foundry that
# delivers mail. A mistake here is a message in somebody's inbox, and there is
# no unsend.
#
# ⚠ DELIBERATE DEVIATION FROM `FOUNDRY_SECRET_SOURCE`. The Graph client secret is
# read from this file rather than from Key Vault, because that was asked for
# explicitly ("keep all the secrets in the .env file only for now"). Production
# should move it to the vault path the other credentials use. Nothing depends on
# it staying here: the secret is read in exactly one module
# (`services/mail.service.ts`, enforced by `mail-secret-single-reader.test.ts`),
# and that one place is where a vault lookup would go.
#
# ⚠ NOT the same thing as `MAIL_API_KEY`, which is the SIMULATED mail service's
# workspace secret. Two mail credentials, one real and one fake. The `MAIL_GRAPH_`
# prefix is what keeps them apart — do not consolidate them.
#
# Two things must be done in Azure before any of this works, and neither is
# visible from a green health check:
#
#   1. Grant the app registration the **`Mail.Send` APPLICATION permission**
#      (not delegated) on Microsoft Graph, and have a tenant admin **grant admin
#      consent**. Without consent the token exchange succeeds and `sendMail`
#      returns 403 — a working credential that cannot send.
#   2. Scope the app with an **Exchange application access policy** limited to
#      `MAIL_SENDER_UPN` (`New-ApplicationAccessPolicy`). Without it the app can
#      send as EVERY mailbox in the tenant. The permission is tenant-wide by
#      design; this policy is what narrows it to one sender.
#
# All four below are required. Unset any of them and the endpoint answers 424
# naming every one that is missing (it does not fail silently, and it does not
# report healthy).
MAIL_GRAPH_TENANT_ID=
MAIL_GRAPH_CLIENT_ID=
# `_VALUE` suffix matches AZURE_VAULT_CLIENT_SECRET_VALUE. Keep the name.
MAIL_GRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET_VALUE=
# The mailbox messages are sent AS. Must be the mailbox the access policy scopes
# the app to, and must be a real licensed mailbox — Graph cannot send as a group.
MAIL_SENDER_UPN=

# Comma-separated domains an EXPLICITLY NAMED recipient may be on.
# ⚠ Empty means the person who started the run and NOBODY ELSE. There is no
# wildcard value, deliberately: the absence of configuration is not consent to
# email the world. The requester is always permitted regardless of this list —
# "email me the result" works on a deployment that has allowlisted nothing.
MAIL_ALLOWED_RECIPIENT_DOMAINS=

# ---- Optional; every one has a working default ----
# Kill switch. ⚠ ABSENT MEANS ON. Only the literal `false` disables delivery, in
# which case the endpoint answers 409 saying so. A kill switch has to be switched
# to kill — defaulting it off would refuse every send on a fully configured
# deployment, citing a variable the operator was never told to add.
# MAIL_ENABLED=false
# Body limit in UTF-8 BYTES, not characters (a 200k-character report is not 200k
# bytes). Default 262144.
# MAIL_MAX_BODY_BYTES=262144
# `to` and `cc` counted together, after case-insensitive dedup. Default 10.
# MAIL_MAX_RECIPIENTS=10
# Where the registered tool's URL points: this server as reached by the STEP
# EXECUTOR, not as reached by a browser. Default http://localhost:4000.
# MAIL_SELF_BASE=http://localhost:4000

# ── Corporate CA for TLS-intercepting proxies (20260824-211547-docker-ca-trust) ──
#
# Only needed on a network that re-signs TLS with a private root CA (Zscaler and
# similar). Symptom without it: EVERY image build fails with
# UNABLE_TO_GET_ISSUER_CERT_LOCALLY (Node), CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED (pip) or
# "PKIX path building failed".
#
# ⚠ LEAVE BOTH UNSET ANYWHERE ELSE. Empty means NO corporate CA, and an image
# built that way is identical to one built before this feature existed. Setting
# them is never required; the feature is opt-in by design.
#
# Usage:
#   ./scripts/stage-corp-ca.sh                       # copies the cert into infra/certs/
#   CORP_CA_FILE=corp-ca.pem                         # then set this
#   docker compose --env-file .env -f infra/docker-compose.yml build
#
# ⚠ RUN COMPOSE FROM THE REPO ROOT WITH `--env-file .env`. Compose interpolates
# ${VAR} from the directory holding the compose file (infra/), not from the
# current directory, and there is no infra/.env. Without the flag CORP_CA_FILE
# expands to EMPTY, the build proceeds with no trust, and then fails at the first
# fetch looking exactly like the original bug.
#
# The name of the staged certificate inside infra/certs/. A bare filename — a
# value containing "/" is refused. Empty (the default) means no corporate CA.
# CORP_CA_FILE=corp-ca.pem
#
# Where the staging script reads the certificate FROM on this workstation. Only
# read by scripts/stage-corp-ca.sh; never by a build. Default $HOME/zscaler-ca.pem.
# CORP_CA_SOURCE=/path/to/your/corporate-root-ca.pem

# ======================================================================
# Step-callback payload bounds — specs/20260819-045902-step-payload-bounds
# ======================================================================
#
# ⚠ THE CAP LADDER, and the ORDER is the design rather than any one number.
# A payload too large for the TOOL it is addressed to must be refused BY THAT
# TOOL, with its own specific sentence. So every cap below sits above the one
# under it by a wide margin:
#
#   llm_complete's `input`        32,000 chars  → LLM_COMPLETE_MAX_INPUT_CHARS (server)
#   foundry_email_send's `body`     262,144 B   → MAIL_MAX_BODY_BYTES (above)
#   a step callback's whole body  5,242,880 B   → STEP_CALLBACK_MAX_BYTES (below)
#   every other route                   2 MB   → hardcoded in app.ts, unchanged
#
# The incident: a tool returned 7.19 MiB, the next step inlined all of it, and a
# 2 MB process-wide cap answered "request entity too large" — two hops upstream
# of ops-api's "input is 7,539,026 characters; the limit is 32,000. Summarise or
# filter upstream", which is the actual diagnosis. The coarse limit fired where
# the specific one had something useful to say.

# The whole body of a POST to /api/steps/* or /api/executions/*. Refused on
# Content-Length BEFORE the body is buffered, with the size, the limit, the
# execution and a remedy. Default 5242880 (5 MB).
#
# ⚠ It does NOT admit the 8,401,233-byte body from the reported incident, and
# that is correct — that body was bound to a 32,000-character parameter, so no
# value here makes sending it right. Raising this until a replay gets through
# INVERTS the design; the run is fixed by reducing the payload upstream with a
# `transform` (or a `code` step for totals), not by widening the transport.
# STEP_CALLBACK_MAX_BYTES=5242880

# SOFT. A tool response over this adds ONE warning and the step still SUCCEEDS.
# Default 1048576 (1 MB).
#
# ⚠ It must never become a refusal: a large read followed by a reducing
# `transform` is a correct flow, and a hard cap here would have failed the BTO
# run at `fetch` instead of at `report` — not an improvement. Nothing truncates.
# TOOL_RESPONSE_WARN_BYTES=1048576

# ---- llm_complete (served by the server since 2026-10-30; was ops-api) ----
# The tightest cap in the ladder and the one whose message the incident lost:
# llm_complete refuses a longer `input` with a 422 naming both numbers. Default
# 32000.
#
# ⚠ Raising this is NOT a way to summarise a big dataset. 7,539,026 characters is
# ~1.9M tokens, past the context window of every model this gateway serves — the
# cap stands in front of a wall no configuration moves. A `code` step reduces the
# rows and llm_complete narrates the result.
# LLM_COMPLETE_MAX_INPUT_CHARS=32000   (OPS_LLM_MAX_INPUT_CHARS is still read as a fallback)
# The origin third parties reach this deployment's API at, used ONLY to print a
# complete example URL on a flow's "Run it from your own systems" panel.
# ⚠ Optional. When unset the panel prints the PATH and says the host is missing,
# rather than assembling one from the browser's origin — which is the WEB app,
# not the API, so a guessed URL would look right and 404.
PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=

# Foundry flow-runner (Pi workflows). Any long random value; the server and the runner share it.
FLOW_RUNNER_SECRET=

# Forge, the builder agent, runs in the `forge` container (packages/forge). The server reaches it with this secret
# (at least 16 characters; generate one with `openssl rand -hex 24`). The forge container refuses to start without it.
FORGE_HOST_SECRET=
# Optional: where Forge may go besides nothing. Defaults to the host of LLM_BASE_URL.
# FORGE_EGRESS_ALLOW=llm-gateway.example.com:443

# Connectors (packages/connector — the `connector` and `connector-egress` containers, started by every compose up).
# Both secrets are required (generate each with `openssl rand -hex 24`); the containers refuse to start without them.
PLUGINS_CONNECTOR_URL=http://connector:4010
PLUGINS_CONNECTOR_SECRET=
PLUGINS_EGRESS_SECRET=
